<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diogenes the Cynic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 412–<a href="/years/323-bce">323 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Diogenes of Sinope</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diogenes the Cynic (c. 412–323 BCE) was a Greek philosopher born in Sinope on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and the best-known figure of the Cynic school. Exiled from his home city, he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Corinth in deliberate poverty, reportedly sleeping in a large jar, and mocked social convention to show that virtue needed only self-sufficiency. Anecdotes about him, including a meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, were collected by Diogenes Laertius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diogenes-the-cynic.jpg" alt="Diogenes the Cynic" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diogenes_Mosaic_R%C3%B6misch-Germanisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
